  "query_preview": "..\nNext 7 Days..\n\nAnother morning of patchy fog is forecast across the CWA. The \nhighest chance of seeing viability reductions wil be in low lying\nareas, river valleys, and locations that still remain moist after\nthe early week precip. Isolated areas of fog are possible again \nFriday AM, especially in low lying areas in the Miles City area. \n\nAn area of weak ridging will remain over the Northern Rockies for\nthe next few days. This will keep skies mostly clear allowing for\ngood diurnal swings, including highs above average. The normal\nhigh for this time of year in most of our population centers is\naround 50F, however highs in the upper 50s to low 60s will be\ncommonplace for the foreseeable future. As a high amplitude ridge\nbuilds in Saturday and Sunday, some locations may even have a\nchance to touch some 70s and record highs. \n\nThe most impactful weather to speak about is the potential for \nsome breezy conditions in the western parts of our CWA late this \nweek and into the weekend. A weak shortwave will push through \nMontana Friday which will come with mainly dry conditions. A few \nsnow showers are possible in the mountains and a stray shower is\npossible near and west of Billings Friday AM. Otherwise, mostly \nclear skies and no accumulation is anticipated. What this wave \nwill bring through is slightly higher 700mb wind speeds. The GFS \nand ECMWF both show the highest 700mb speeds in our forecast area \nover south-central MT, which are only being depicted in the 30s \nkts range. The RRFS has values even lower in the 20s kts. This \ncould allow for some breezy conditions for Big Timber and \nHarlowton, but nothing like we so often experience this time of \nyear. 30s to 40s mph gusts are possible Friday afternoon but gusts\nof 50+ mph seem very unlikely.\n\nSaturday morning, behind the weak wave, there is a signal that\nLivingston could turn on its gap winds. The GFS is showing \ndelta-P values between IDA-LWT of 12-14 mb between 00z and 18z \nSaturday with the GFS Ensemble even keeping values in the 9-11mb \nrange. Due to possibility of some high clouds, the surface \ninversion may not be super strong which could limit how strong the\ngap winds get. Local guidance is showing a 40-50% chance of wind \ngusts in the 50s around Sunrise Saturday so this will be something\nwe will keep an eye on as more high-res models come in. \n\nLooking ahead to next week, it appears that the overall pattern\nfor our region remains relatively quiet. Long range models do \nshow more weak short waves pass near the our CWA but non of which\nseem to be more impactful than some breezy winds or a few \nshowers. Overall, the strat of november is looking to be quite \nsimilar to what we saw most of October. WMR",
